APPENDIX A: STOCHASTIC APPROXIMATION OF THE HESSIAN SPECTRAL RADIUS

A.1 Problem Statement

Theorem 8.2 (Lyapunov Stability) requires checking that a proposed architectural mutation does not increase the largest Hessian eigenvalue beyond a tolerance \delta. For a model with P parameters, the full Hessian \mathbf{H} \in \mathbb{R}^{P \times P} cannot be stored, let alone diagonalised. The same difficulty arises in the Spectral Budget Rule of §8.5, where the Hessian trace \operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{H}) must be estimated at each meta‑optimisation step.

A.2 Hessian‑Vector Products via Automatic Differentiation

The fundamental operation that makes large‑scale Hessian estimation possible is the Hessian‑vector product (HVP), which can be computed in O(P) time without ever forming the full matrix [1]. Given a loss \mathcal{L}(\mathbf{w}) and an arbitrary vector \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^P,

\mathbf{H}\mathbf{v} = \nabla_{\mathbf{w}} \big( \langle \nabla_{\mathbf{w}}\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{w}), \,\mathbf{v} \rangle \big).

Concretely, one first computes the gradient \mathbf{g} = \nabla_{\mathbf{w}}\mathcal{L}, then performs a second backward pass on the scalar quantity \mathbf{g}^\top \mathbf{v}. This is now a standard primitive in all major automatic‑differentiation frameworks and is well established in the literature: R‑operator implementations make the computation efficient for both fully‑connected and convolutional architectures [3–5]. With the HVP in hand, both \lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{H}) and \operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{H}) become accessible.

A.3 Power Iteration for the Largest Eigenvalue

The power method iterates

\mathbf{v}_{k+1} = \frac{\mathbf{H}\mathbf{v}_k}{\|\mathbf{H}\mathbf{v}_k\|},
\qquad \mathbf{v}_0 \sim \text{Uniform}(\mathbb{S}^{P-1}),

and after K steps the Rayleigh quotient

\hat{\lambda}_{\max} = \mathbf{v}_K^\top \mathbf{H} \mathbf{v}_K

converges to the true \lambda_{\max} at a geometric rate |\lambda_2 / \lambda_1|^K. For trained transformers, the spectral gap is typically substantial: the dominant eigenvalue is separated from the bulk by a factor that makes K \in [10, 50] sufficient [8]. The Edge of Stochastic Stability literature confirms that the largest Hessian eigenvalue is the appropriate diagnostic, with SGD dynamically tuning it to hover near 2/\eta; the Stochastic Sharpness Gap model quantifies the separation between the full‑batch and mini‑batch sharpness, providing precise variance bounds for power‑iteration estimates under realistic training noise [8].

A.4 Stochastic Lanczos and Spectral Density Estimation

When more than the top eigenvalue is required—for instance when estimating the Hessian trace for the Spectral Budget—the randomised Lanczos algorithm builds a small tridiagonal projection \mathbf{T}_m \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times m} whose extremal eigenvalues converge rapidly to those of \mathbf{H} [4, 5]. The algorithm is implemented in PyTorch (the hessian‑eigenthings package), has been validated in saddle‑free Newton methods [4], and has been extended with stochastic Lanczos quadrature for on‑the‑fly learning‑rate adaptation [9].

For the trace, Hutchinson’s stochastic estimator is used:

\operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{H}) \approx \frac{1}{R} \sum_{r=1}^R \mathbf{z}_r^\top \mathbf{H} \mathbf{z}_r,
\qquad \mathbf{z}_r \sim \text{Rademacher},

where each term \mathbf{z}_r^\top \mathbf{H} \mathbf{z}_r is a single HVP followed by a dot product. The estimator is unbiased and its variance scales as O(1/R). This is the standard method for flatness‑aware regularisation and PAC‑Bayesian Hessian‑trace penalisation [14, 18]. NeurIPS 2024 work explicitly proves a PAC‑Bayes bound that depends on \operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{H}) and provides a two‑point noise‑injection estimator that eliminates first‑order Taylor variance [18].

A.5 Practical Overhead

A single power iteration requires two backward passes (one to compute \mathbf{g}, one for the HVP). For a transformer of 10^9 parameters, this adds approximately the cost of two gradient computations per meta‑step. Since the meta‑optimiser evaluates a proposed mutation far less frequently than the base training loop—perhaps every few hundred training steps—the amortised overhead is under 2 % of total compute. The Lanczos and Hutchinson estimators have similar complexity.

