APPENDIX C: DYNAMIC SCALING EXPONENTS AND REGIME‑SHIFT DETECTION

C.1 Effective NTK Rank as a Complexity Diagnostic

Proposition 8.3 defines the effective dimension of the NTK:

d_{\text{eff}} = \frac{(\operatorname{Tr} \mathbf{K})^2}{\|\mathbf{K}\|_F^2} = \frac{(\sum_i \mu_i)^2}{\sum_i \mu_i^2},

where \mu_i are the eigenvalues of the empirical NTK Gram matrix \mathbf{K}. This quantity, also known as the effective rank, is a scalar summary of spectral concentration [4, 12].

Theoretical foundation. Shukla (2025) proved a constant‑limit law for the NTK effective rank: for i.i.d. data and the infinite‑width NTK kernel k,

\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}\big[ r_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{K}_n) \big] = \frac{\mathbb{E}[k(x,x)]^2}{\mathbb{E}[k(x,x')^2]} =: r_\infty,

with sub‑Gaussian concentration [12]. Moreover, when the finite‑width NTK deviates by O_p(m^{-1/2}) in operator norm (width m), the effective rank changes by only O_p(m^{-1/2}), establishing finite‑width stability [12]. On CIFAR‑10 with ResNet‑20/56 (widths 16–32) across n \in \{10^3, 5\times10^4\}, the observed r_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.0–1.3 with slopes \approx 0 in n, consistent with the constant‑limit theory [12].

Link to scaling exponents. From approximation theory in reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces, Bordelon et al. (ICLR 2025) proved that feature learning can nearly double the compute‑scaling exponent for tasks that lie outside the RKHS of the initial NTK, compared to lazy (kernel) training. The effective rank governs this steepening: as d_{\text{eff}} increases, more kernel eigenmodes contribute, and the approximation exponent \alpha grows. The ICML 2024 Spotlight paper of Anagnostidis et al. demonstrated that adaptive models which change shape during training optimally traverse between static scaling laws, significantly reducing required compute.

C.2 Local Exponent Estimation

Given checkpoints \{ (N_i, D_i, L_i) \}_{i=1}^T collected during training, we fit a power‑law model L \approx B C^{-s} over a sliding window of size W (typically W = 10–20 checkpoints) via log‑linear regression:

\log L_i = \log B - s \log C_i + \varepsilon_i, \quad C_i = 6 N_i D_i.

The fitted slope \hat{s}_t is the instantaneous scaling exponent at window t. This quantity is tracked alongside d_{\text{eff}}.

C.3 Change‑Point Detection for Regime Shifts

We employ the Cumulative Sum (CUSUM) algorithm, the foundational frequentist method for sequential change‑point detection [5]. Given a univariate time series x_t (either d_{\text{eff}}^{-1}(t) or \hat{s}_t), the CUSUM statistic is

S_t = \max\left(0, \; S_{t-1} + x_t - \mu_0 - \kappa \right),

where \mu_0 is the estimated pre‑change mean and \kappa is a drift parameter that controls sensitivity. An alarm is raised when S_t exceeds a calibrated threshold h.

For the self‑improvement meta‑loop, the NN‑CUSUM variant of Chen and Xie (2022/2024), which trains a neural discriminator to distinguish pre‑ and post‑change distributions before feeding its output into a CUSUM recursion, provides superior sensitivity in high‑dimensional settings [5, 13]. A regime shift is declared when two conditions are met simultaneously:

1. The relative change in d_{\text{eff}} exceeds 15–20 % (sustained across three consecutive windows).
2. The CUSUM statistic for \hat{s}_t crosses the threshold h, indicating a persistent change in the local scaling exponent.

Upon declaration, the model triggers a re‑estimation of \alpha and \beta by fitting the full scaling law L(N,D) = E + A/N^\alpha + B/D^\beta to an expanded validation sweep of (N, D) pairs, and the compute allocation and Spectral Budget are updated accordingly.

