APPENDIX E: LIPSCHITZ ENFORCEMENT FOR DYNAMICALLY GROWING ARCHITECTURES

E.1 Spectral Normalisation as a Lipschitz Constraint

Theorem 8.3 requires each layer to satisfy a Lipschitz constant L \le 1 to guarantee that the composition of arbitrarily many layers remains 1‑Lipschitz. For linear layers with weight matrix \mathbf{W}, the spectral norm \|\mathbf{W}\|_2 = \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}) is the exact Lipschitz constant. Spectral normalisation replaces \mathbf{W} by \mathbf{W} / \|\mathbf{W}\|_2, which can be enforced efficiently during training via a single power iteration per forward pass; this is now a standard tf.keras.layers primitive [16]. For attention layers, the full mapping is more complex, but per‑component spectral normalisation on each projection matrix \mathbf{W}_Q, \mathbf{W}_K, \mathbf{W}_V, \mathbf{W}_O combined with the 1‑Lipschitz property of softmax and layer normalisation is sufficient to bound the overall Lipschitz constant.

E.2 Contractive ODE Flows

For the continuous‑depth ODE model \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = F(\mathbf{x}(t), t), the Transformer Flow Approximation Theorem of Fein‑Ashley (2025) proves that under standard Lipschitz continuity assumptions, token representations converge uniformly to the unique ODE solution as the number of layers grows [7]. If F satisfies a one‑sided Lipschitz condition with a negative constant:

\langle F(\mathbf{x}, t) - F(\mathbf{y}, t), \;\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y} \rangle \le -\nu \|\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y}\|^2, \qquad \nu > 0,

then the dynamics are contractive:

\|\mathbf{x}(t) - \mathbf{y}(t)\| \le e^{-\nu t} \|\mathbf{x}(0) - \mathbf{y}(0)\|,

guaranteeing that perturbations decay exponentially and that gradients never explode [7]. The contractivity constant \nu can be monitored at training time via the log‑norm of the Jacobian. When the meta‑optimiser extends the integration time (i.e., increases effective depth), the contractivity condition provides a formal guarantee that stability is preserved.

E.3 Lipschitz‑Bounded Equilibrium Networks

The Lipschitz‑Bounded Equilibrium Network framework of Revay, Wang, and Manchester (2020) provides an alternative parameterisation for residual and implicit‑depth models in which a tight Lipschitz bound is maintained during training via unconstrained optimisation, requiring no projections or barrier functions [15]. The framework establishes novel connections with convex optimisation, operator splitting on non‑Euclidean spaces, and contracting neural ODEs, and has been validated on image classification tasks with accurate Lipschitz bounds and improved adversarial robustness [15].

E.4 Integration with the Meta‑Loop

When the self‑improvement meta‑optimiser proposes adding a new layer, the new layer is initialised with spectral normalisation and a Lipschitz bound \le 1. The meta‑loop verifies, via stochastic power iteration on the new layer’s Jacobian, that the composition’s overall Lipschitz constant does not exceed 1 (or, for the ODE variant, that the one‑sided Lipschitz constant remains negative). This check is computationally cheap—it requires one HVP per new layer—and provides a formal guarantee against gradient explosion during self‑modification.

